# The hub's environment. Copy to `.env.hub` and fill in; `make dev` and
# `make hub-dev` need it, and the hub refuses to start without the secrets.
# `.env.hub` is gitignored; this template is re-admitted by name.
#
# Generate every secret below with the commands shown -- `make hub-secrets`
# prints the whole set at once and writes NOTHING.

# ── the edge ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The OIDC issuer, verbatim, NO TRAILING SLASH: instances compare their
# VENONA_OIDC_ISSUER against the id_token's `iss` byte-for-byte.
HUB_PUBLIC_URL=http://localhost:8180

# ── auth ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Session cookie signing key, >= 32 chars.        uv run hub secrets
HUB_SESSION_SECRET=

# Fernet key sealing instance mgmt tokens at rest. Same command. ⚠️ Losing it
# strands every sealed token -- re-register each instance with
# `hub instances set-mgmt-token`. It lives ONLY here, never in the database,
# which is what keeps a database dump free of usable credentials.
HUB_SEAL_KEY=

# Bearer for /admin/* (instance registration, provisioning). Same command.
HUB_ADMIN_TOKEN=

# RS256 signing key for id_tokens, base64-encoded PEM.   uv run hub keys new
HUB_OIDC_SIGNING_KEY=

# The hub's OWN Google OAuth client (the hub authenticates people with
# Google; instances authenticate with the hub). Create at
# https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials with the redirect URI
# EXACTLY  {HUB_PUBLIC_URL}/auth/callback
HUB_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
HUB_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Who may open the server-rendered operator page at /admin (request triage).
# Comma-separated emails, matched against the signed-in hub session. Empty
# means the pages refuse everyone; the CLI and admin API are unaffected.
HUB_OPERATOR_EMAILS=

# ── optional knobs (defaults shown) ──────────────────────────────────
# HUB_SESSION_TTL_SECONDS=1209600
# HUB_COOKIE_SECURE=1            # set 0 for plain-http localhost
# HUB_POLL_INTERVAL_SECONDS=300
# HUB_POLL_ENABLED=1
# HUB_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://venona:venona-local@127.0.0.1:5432/hub
# HUB_SITE_URL=                  # a front site publishing /version.json

# ── billing, optional (ADR 0209, 0212) ───────────────────────────────
# BOTH OR NEITHER. The app refuses to start on a partial configuration:
# a secret key with no webhook secret would take money and never hear
# that it had. Leave them empty to run a hub that takes no payments,
# which is the right state for every local hub.
#
# The webhook secret is per ENDPOINT and per MODE -- `stripe listen`
# prints a different one from the dashboard's, and a test-mode secret in
# front of live events refuses every one of them as a bad signature.
#
# Local rehearsal:
#   stripe listen --forward-to localhost:8180/billing/webhook
HUB_STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
HUB_STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
#
# Prices are per instance, not per hub: `hub instances pricing <slug>`
# creates each domain's own product. Stripe's portal lists a customer's
# subscriptions by product name, so two domains sharing one product are
# indistinguishable to a reader deciding which to cancel.
#
# THE PORTAL CONFIGURATION ID (bpc_...). Optional, and it matters when
# the Stripe account is shared with anything else: with this unset the
# portal falls back to the account default, which belongs to whoever set
# it last. Their cancel policy, their plan list, their branding, and a
# deep link that 400s the day they turn a flow off.
# HUB_STRIPE_PORTAL_CONFIGURATION=

# ── the launch switch ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# NOT AN ENVIRONMENT VARIABLE. Whether a signed-in stranger is granted a
# domain, and whether each domain charges to read, are thrown on
# /admin/settings and kept in the hub's own database (migration 0008).
# HUB_TRIAL_DAYS=3
